心理和神经疾病的关联。但目前我们对高端的超心盲症的了解比心盲症要少。所以我们了解不多，但我确实见过一些人，他们说他们可以重播整部电影，他们向我发誓他们的意识体验就像在 iPad 上观看一样。就是那么清晰。现在，我绝对做不到这一点。所以，这对我来说很难相信，但我只见过一两个人这么说，我们还没有机会详细研究他们。但似乎确实有人能做到这一点。所以，是的，这与完全没有意象有很大不同。

45
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:50.000
Does AFantasia exist only for visual images? I guess my question is, is it possible to have the equivalent of AFantasia for other senses, say being unable to hear music in your mind or recall a smell or a texture? Yeah, great question, Kim. Yeah, absolutely. So it does, it's not just visual. You can have full multisensory Aphantasia. So we've done some research where we measured a few thousand people
心盲症只存在于视觉意象中吗？我想我的问题是，是否可能在其他感官上也存在心盲症的等价物，比如无法在脑海中听到音乐，或回忆起气味或质地？是的，好问题，Kim。是的，绝对可以。所以它不仅仅是视觉的。你可以拥有完全的多感官心盲症。我们进行了一些研究，测量了几千人，

46
00:27:50.000 --> 00:28:19.000
and ran a sort of clustering analysis, a bunch of different clustering analysis to see the groupings. And we found that, yeah, most people who had AFantasia tended to have this full multisensory Aphantasia. But there was also a significant group that just had pure visual. And then we found all the other groupings you could imagine, but in very small quantities or proportions. So you could have a pure auditory, Afantasia, you could have auditory and visual,
并运行了一种聚类分析，一堆不同的聚类分析来查看分组。我们发现，是的，大多数患有心盲症的人往往患有这种完全的多感官心盲症。但也有一个显著的群体只是纯视觉的。然后我们发现了你能想象到的所有其他分组，但数量或比例非常小。所以你可以有纯听觉心盲症，你可以有听觉和视觉，

47
00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:55.000
but they were very small groups compared to the sort of larger groups of pure visual or pure multisensory across all the sensors. And so there is a bias there in that the databases and the people we test have heard about Afantasia as a visual thing. So of course there's going to be more people on the database that have visual, Afantasia, but the clustering do exist there. and you can also drill down and there's people that have sort of auditory a Fantasia and then there's also a subgroup which might be able to imagine music and sounds
但与纯视觉或跨所有感官的纯多感官的大群体相比，它们是非常小的群体。所以那里存在一种偏差，因为数据库和我们测试的人听说过心盲症是一种视觉事物。所以当然，数据库中会有更多患有视觉心盲症的人，但聚类确实存在。你也可以深入挖掘，有些人患有听觉心盲症，然后还有一个亚组可能能够想象音乐和声音，

48
00:28:55.000 --> 00:29:33.000
but don't have an sort of inner mind's voice if you're like when they read they don't hear the mental version of words being read to them they can't talk to themselves and that's actually started to grow into its own sort of subfield now of sort of the inner workings of the minds and the inner voice but absolutely yeah imagery can be a across any of the senses, which is pretty interesting in itself. And I often have thought about this, we've written about this a bit, that you don't really have a version of multi-sensory blindness, for example. I can't think of an individual in history that has had no sensory abilities across all
但没有所谓的“内心声音”，如果你愿意的话，当他们阅读时，他们听不到文字被读给他们的心理版本，他们无法对自己说话，这实际上已经开始发展成为自己的一个子领域，即思维的内在运作和内在声音，但绝对是的，意象可以跨越任何感官，这本身就非常有趣。我经常思考这个问题，我们对此写过一点，你并没有多感官失明之类的版本。我想不出历史上有什么人拥有跨越所有感官的感官能力缺失。

49
00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:59.000
their senses. No sight, no sound, no touch, no taste, no smell. Like, I don't think we've ever had that. But the fact that you can have that in Afantasia already tells you something interesting about the differences between mental imagery across the senses and sensory perception, that there is something more central going on there, that you can have all these things not existing together. Let me switch topics here a little bit.
他们的感官。没有视觉、没有听觉、没有触觉、没有味觉、没有嗅觉。我觉得我们以前从未遇到过这种情况。但事实上，在心盲症（Afantasia）中你可以拥有这种体验，这本身就揭示了感官心理意象与感官知觉之间差异的一些有趣之处，即在大脑中存在着更核心的机制，使得这些感官体验可以同时缺失。让我稍微转换一下话题。

50
00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:25.000
I know you've studied the connection between Afantasia and creativity. What have you found? What is the relationship? Yeah, so we've done a bunch of research on this. We haven't yet published them. It's kind of a bit of a pipeline in the lab, a clogged up pipeline of, publications at present. But in some, we found that there was no clear differences between those that have a Fantasia and those that have imagery in the sort of the classic ways of trying to measure
我知道你研究过心盲症与创造力之间的联系。你发现了什么？它们之间是什么关系？是的，我们在这方面做了很多研究。我们还没有发表这些成果。目前实验室里有一堆积压的论文等待发表。但在一些研究中，我们发现，在衡量创造力的经典方式上，心盲症患者与拥有心理意象的人之间并没有明显的差异。

51
00:30:25.000 --> 00:31:06.000
creativity. And this is this divergent thinking, tasks like, you know, here's a brick. How many different ways can you, could you use the brick? And then you have independent people score that on, you know, how creative it is and also how many different uses and things like that. So despite what people think and say that A Fantasia is holding back their creativity at work or something. The data doesn't really support that because we didn't see any differences. And then having said that, you know, we've had A Fantasia visual art exhibitions. Ed Capable, who co-founded Pixar and ran that and then ran Disney Studios for many years. A friend of mine, he has A Fantasia.
这就是发散性思维，比如任务是：给你一块砖头，你能想出多少种不同的用法？然后由独立评分员根据创造力程度以及用途的多样性等指标进行打分。所以，尽管人们认为心盲症会阻碍工作中的创造力，但数据并不支持这一点，因为我们没有观察到任何差异。话虽如此，我们举办过心盲症视觉艺术展。皮克斯（Pixar）的联合创始人、曾长期执掌皮克斯和迪士尼工作室的艾德·卡特姆（Ed Catmull）也是我的朋友，他就是一位心盲症患者。

52
00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:36.000
So he was crucial early on and coming up with some of the ideas and methods for doing computer design. using these sort of geometric shapes to define a square or a sphere. And he said that if he could visualize the shapes, he probably wouldn't have come up with this mathematical geometry, these little triangles and things to describe these shapes. And so he almost credits A Fantasia and helping him with some of these breakthroughs in these sort of 3D designs. Also one of their top animators,
他在早期构思计算机设计的一些理念和方法时起到了至关重要的作用，比如使用几何形状来定义正方形或球体。他说，如果他能进行视觉化想象，他可能就不会想出这种数学几何方法，即用这些小三角形之类的东西来描述形状。因此，他几乎将这些3D设计领域的突破归功于心盲症。此外，他们的一位顶级动画师，

53
00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:59.000
I'm blanking on the gentleman's name, but he was one of the top, the person who did a lot of the little mermaid designs for the characters. He has Afantasia. And so I don't want anyone to think that, you know, if you have Afentasia, it's some barrier to being creative or starting an enterprise or a business or any of these things. It does not seem to be the case. So I think it's important to get that out there.
我一时想不起这位先生的名字，但他是一位顶尖人物，负责了《小美人鱼》中许多角色的设计。他也是心盲症患者。所以我不想让任何人认为，如果你有心盲症，这就是发挥创造力、创业或从事任何事业的障碍。事实似乎并非如此。所以我认为把这一点传达出来很重要。

54
00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:25.000
Yeah, and I didn't want to give the impression either that A Fantasia is some kind of a disorder that needs to be fixed. I mean, it seems from what I've been reading anyway, and you would know better than I, that people who have it don't feel that they're in any way held back in. in life, but I'm wondering if there are interventions, so if somebody said, yeah, I'd like to know what it's like to have visual internal life. Is there anything that can be done to help with that?
是的，我也不想给人留下心盲症是一种需要“修复”的疾病的印象。我的意思是，根据我的阅读，而且你比我更了解，那些患有心盲症的人并不觉得他们在生活中受到了任何阻碍。但我很好奇是否有干预措施，如果有人说：“是的，我想体验一下拥有视觉内心世界是什么感觉”，有什么办法可以帮助他们吗？

55
00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:56.000
Like I said, I mentioned earlier, we've done the study using that sort of that type of brain stimulation where we have up or down regulated imagery strength. All those participants had some form of imagery to begin with. Now, separately, we've done a five-day experiment where we had people come into the lab and practice their imagery every day for one hour for five days in a row, and while they were practicing, you know, we were measuring it using that binocular ivory technique. And we didn't really see any improvement over that time.
就像我之前提到的，我们做过一项研究，利用那种脑刺激技术来上调或下调意象强度。所有参与者在实验开始时都具备某种形式的意象能力。另外，我们还做过一个为期五天的实验，让参与者每天到实验室练习一小时意象，连续五天。在他们练习时，我们使用双眼竞争技术进行测量。但在那段时间里，我们并没有看到明显的改善。

56
00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:21.000
Now, that doesn't mean it's not trainable. The thing with brain stimulation is it's not magically going to tell you how to create a mental image. In the same way that if you wanted to learn a new language, let's say you can't speak Chinese and you want to learn Chinese, while I can stimulate the language areas of your brain, and that would improve your performance somewhat, it's not going to magically give you the ability and the knowledge to speak Chinese. So I think the answer may be in combining practice
但这并不意味着它无法训练。脑刺激的问题在于，它不会神奇地告诉你如何创造心理意象。就像如果你想学习一门新语言，比如你不会说中文，你想学中文，虽然我可以刺激你大脑中的语言区，这会提高你的表现，但它不会神奇地赋予你说中文的能力和知识。所以我认为答案可能在于将练习与脑刺激结合起来。

57
00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:51.000
with brain stimulation. Now, there is an emerging trend that has not, to my knowledge, been studied scientifically yet of this image streaming technique that came from photography where people would first have their eyes open and look at a room and then verbally out loud describe the visual details as much as possible. And they do this over and over. And this came from photography where they were trying to design and the right way to set up a photograph.
目前出现了一种趋势，据我所知尚未经过科学研究，即源自摄影领域的“意象流”（image streaming）技术。人们首先睁开眼睛观察一个房间，然后尽可能大声地用语言描述视觉细节。他们反复这样做。这源于摄影界，当时他们试图设计和确定布置照片的最佳方式。

58
00:33:51.000 --> 00:34:31.000
And then the idea is then you close your eyes and try and do it by memory. And it's important, apparently, that you do this out loud. So there's a few groups online, in particular on YouTube now who are talking about that as a way to train mental imagery or give people mental imagery experience. Now, I know some people who have tried that and they haven't really noticed much difference. Some people claim that they have. And so again, it may be the case that some people who have Afentasia have some residual imagery and that can be trained up and part of that equation might be simply teaching people what to pay attention to because they don't even know what imagery is in the first place.
然后，接下来的步骤是闭上眼睛，尝试通过记忆来完成。显然，大声说出来很重要。现在网上有一些群体，特别是在YouTube上，正在讨论将其作为训练心理意象或让人们获得心理意象体验的方法。我知道有些人尝试过，但并没有感觉到太大差异。有些人声称他们有效果。所以，再次强调，可能有些心盲症患者确实残留了一些意象能力，可以通过训练提升，而其中的一部分可能仅仅是教人们如何去注意，因为他们甚至不知道什么是意象。

59
00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:56.000
I should also mention there's two published case studies, they're retrospective studies, from people who have been part of psychedelic research. At least two of those individuals, and by retrospective they won't set out beforehand to study mental imagery, they kind of discovered this along the way. And so it does rely on their memory of what the imagery was like before they took part
我还应该提到有两个已发表的案例研究，它们是回顾性研究，对象是参与过迷幻药研究的人。至少有两个人，之所以说是回顾性，是因为他们事先并没有打算研究心理意象，而是在过程中偶然发现的。因此，这依赖于他们对参与迷幻药研究之前意象体验的记忆。

60
00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:17.000
in the psychedelic research. And they say that not only did they have the experience of imagery during the effect of the molecule, but that lasted afterwards as well. and that has given them imagery. Now because it's just these two case studies, we have no idea whether these are, you know, two out of a thousand people or would this work for everyone.
他们说，不仅在药物作用期间有了意象体验，而且这种体验在之后也持续存在，这赋予了他们意象能力。由于这只是两个案例研究，我们不知道这是否是千分之一的概率，还是对每个人都有效。

61
00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:54.000
I certainly have talked to people who have taken part in psychedelic research and have Afentasia and it didn't change their imagery. So I don't think it's going to work for everyone. But there's some interesting things possibly emerging there because we know that psychedelics will do a few things, but one of the things it does is put your brain to this very plastic state where it can rewire quite quickly. And so that's very interesting in respect to Afantasia and mental imagery. So just to wrap up, what might studying Afantasia and Hyperfantasia tell us more broadly about how the human mind works?
我确实和一些参与过迷幻药研究的心盲症患者谈过，他们的意象能力并没有改变。所以我认为这不会对每个人都有效。但其中可能出现了一些有趣的现象，因为我们知道迷幻药会产生一些作用，其中之一就是让大脑进入一种高度可塑的状态，可以很快地重连。因此，这在心盲症和心理意象方面非常有趣。最后总结一下，研究心盲症和超意象症（Hyperfantasia）能让我们更广泛地了解人类思维是如何运作的吗？

62
00:35:54.000 --> 00:36:26.000
I think first up, it's a really powerful and nice example of cognitive and neural diversity across the human experience. Another way to phrase all this would be that the format, right? The format of your thoughts does change your life. And it changes the way you think. It changes how much you might donate to a charity. It changes your risk perception. So any time your thoughts will take on the format of a depictive picture in your brain and mind, then that will nudge you and change the way you think,
我认为首先，这是一个关于人类认知和神经多样性的非常有力且美妙的例子。换句话说，思维的格式确实会改变你的生活。它改变了你的思考方式。它改变了你向慈善机构捐款的金额。它改变了你的风险感知。所以，每当你的思想在大脑中以描绘性图像的格式呈现时，它就会引导并改变你的思考方式，

63
00:36:26.000 --> 00:37:03.000
almost like a cognitive bias or a nudge in that language. So that in itself is really interesting. I think also it teaches us about the internal types of consciousness research. I've also always been fascinated by consciousness studies. I think imagery is one of the ways to study internal consciousness. In visual neuroscience, one of the things that's really hard to study is feedback signals. So we know that there's more signals that go backwards, if you like, in the brain than forwards. So forwards means from my retina and my eye, back to visual cortex and up to the higher levels of the brain.
就像认知偏差或语言中的“助推”（nudge）一样。所以这本身就非常有趣。我认为它也让我们了解了内部意识研究。我一直对意识研究很着迷。我认为意象是研究内部意识的方法之一。在视觉神经科学中，很难研究的一件事是反馈信号。我们知道，大脑中向后传递的信号比向前传递的信号多。向前意味着从视网膜和眼睛，传回视觉皮层，再传到大脑的高级区域。

64
00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:33.000
But there's more connections that go backwards in many parts of the brain. We don't know a lot about what those do. And it's very hard to study. But voluntary visual imagery is one of the few cases where you're going to have the eyes closed, have no visual stimulation, have someone imagine something, and then study those feedback signals that are involved in visualizing mental imagery in a very pure isolated format. By that very definition, we stand to learn a lot about sensory perception
但在大脑的许多部分，向后传递的连接更多。我们对它们的功能知之甚少，而且很难研究。但自愿的视觉意象是少数几种情况之一，你可以闭上眼睛，没有任何视觉刺激，让某人想象某物，然后在非常纯粹、孤立的格式下研究参与视觉意象的反馈信号。根据这个定义，我们有望通过研究心理意象来了解很多关于感官知觉的知识。

65
00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:48.000
from studying mental imagery. And if I want to be controversial and probably annoying my colleagues in the vision science community, I would say the key to understanding vision would actually be one of the keys to understanding vision would be visual imagery. And so, yeah, the neurodiversity,
如果我想引起争议，可能会惹恼视觉科学界的同事，我会说理解视觉的关键实际上——或者说关键之一——就是视觉意象。所以，是的，神经多样性、理解反馈、理解意识，所有这些我认为最终都对科学、心理学和神经科学做出了非常重要的贡献。

66
00:37:48.000 --> 00:38:06.000
the understanding feedback, understanding consciousness, all those things I think end up being really important contributions to science in general, to psychology and neuroscience. Well, Dr. Pearson, I want to thank you for joining me. This has been really interesting. I think you're working in a very unusual part of psychology.
好的，皮尔逊博士，感谢你接受我的采访。这非常有趣。我认为你在心理学中一个非常不同寻常的领域工作。

